IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Wills Act 1970 (Western Australia) -- who may make a will and what it may do (ss 6, 7), how it must be signed and
-- witnessed and who may witness (ss 8, 10, 11), what has become of gifts to witnesses (the deleted s 13), how a will is
-- revoked and revived (ss 14, 14A, 15, 16), the other routes to a formally valid will (s 17, s 20, Part XA, Part X
-- ss 32-33, Part XI s 40), and what happens to a gift whose beneficiary dies first (ss 26(1)(b), (g), 27).
--
-- SEAT: the will-maker (the testator) and the beneficiary. Every decision is phrased as one of them would ask it: "Am I old
-- enough to make a will?", "Is my will properly witnessed?", "Does my divorce revoke my will?", "Does a witness lose a
-- gift?", "What becomes of the gift if the beneficiary dies first?". Where the Act hands a judgment to the Supreme Court
-- (Part X, s 40, s 42), the answer is conditional on that judgment, which is asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 4 ("disposition", "will"), s 6, s 7, s 8, s 10(1)-(3), s 11, s 13 (deleted) with the Acts Amendment
-- (Equality of Status) Act 2003 s 211(2) (Other notes, note 1), s 14(1)-(3), s 14A(2)-(4), s 15, s 16(1), s 17, s 20(1),
-- (2)(b), s 26(1)(b), (g), s 27(1), (3), Part XA (s 32B and Annex Art 1, as one fact), s 32(2), s 33(1), s 39(2), s 40(1),
-- (2), (4), s 42(1).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49116), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49116.txt. The deposit names no compilation number; its compilation table ends with
-- the Legal Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2022 s 424 (in force 1 July 2022); the last reprint listed is Reprint 5
-- as at 10 July 2015; the uncommenced provisions table lists the Assisted Reproductive Technology and Surrogacy Act 2025
-- Pt 17 Div 15 (not shown, not encoded). The Act has no regulations that set numbers; its only numbers are the age of 18
-- (ss 7, 40(2)), the 2 witnesses (s 8(c)), and two commencement dates read from the compilation table.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 5 (the Act applies to deaths on or after 1 July 1970 -- every death here is later); s 9 (formalities of a
-- power of appointment); s 10(4) (where the signatures to an alteration go); s 16(2), (3); s 20(1)'s detail beyond one fact,
-- s 20(2)(a), (c), (d), s 21; the construction rules in ss 24, 25, 26(1)(a), (c)-(f), 28, 28A; Part IX; the Annex beyond
-- one fact; ss 41, 43-48 (procedure for and recognition of statutory wills); Part XII s 50 (rectification). Probate and
-- intestacy are the Administration Act 1903; a claim by family against an estate is the Family Provision Act 1972; whether
-- a jointly held home or a superannuation balance passes by the will at all is for the law governing that property; whether
-- Italian law divides a house in Calabria is choice of law, which this Act does not decide (s 20 speaks only to whether a
-- will is "properly executed").
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 1970 (WA)`

§§ `dates the Act turns on`

-- Compilation table: "Acts Amendment (Equality of Status) Act 2003 Pt. 62 ... 1 Jul 2003". That Part deleted s 13.
`the day section 13 was repealed` MEANS Date 1 7 2003

-- Compilation table: "Wills Amendment Act 2007 s. 324 ... 9 Feb 2008". The entry "s. 324" is read (INFERENCE) as ss 3-24,
-- which includes s 12 (s 14(3)(b)), s 13 (s 14A) and s 23 (ss 32, 33). Not confirmed against the Gazette.
`the day the Wills Amendment Act 2007 commenced` MEANS Date 9 2 2008

-- s 7 and s 40(2)(b): "the age of 18 years".
`the age of capacity` MEANS 18

-- s 8(c): "at least 2 witnesses present at the same time".
`the number of witnesses required` MEANS 2

GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE
      `on`      IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`age in completed years` MEANS
    IF     DATE_MONTH `on` GREATER THAN DATE_MONTH `born on`
        OR (DATE_MONTH `on` EQUALS DATE_MONTH `born on` AND DATE_DAY `on` AT LEAST DATE_DAY `born on`)
    THEN DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on`
    ELSE DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on` MINUS 1

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE
      `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is on or after` MEANS Day `a` AT LEAST Day `b`

§§ `6 -- what my will may do`

-- s 6: "A person may, by a will executed or made in a manner required or permitted by this Act -- (a) dispose of property
-- (whether acquired before or after the making of the will) to which at the time of the person's death the person is entitled
-- either at law or in equity; and (b) dispose of property that in exercise of a power of appointment the person is entitled
-- or able to dispose of by will; and (c) appoint a guardian of any infant child of the person."
-- s 4: "disposition includes a gift, devise, bequest or an appointment of property contained in a will".
-- The Act never mentions trusts. A gift to trustees on trust is still a disposition of property, so (INFERENCE) a testamentary
-- trust of property within s 6(a) is a provision a will may make; the trust's terms are general law, not this Act.
DECLARE Provision IS ONE OF
    `a gift of property I own when I die`                      -- s 6(a)
    `a gift of property I acquire after making the will`       -- s 6(a): "whether acquired before or after"
    `a gift on trust of property I own when I die`             -- s 6(a), read with s 4 (INFERENCE: a testamentary trust)
    `an appointment under a power I may exercise by will`      -- s 6(b)
    `a guardian for my infant child`                           -- s 6(c)
    `a gift of property I am not entitled to when I die`       -- outside s 6(a)

@export May my will make this provision (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 6)?
GIVEN `the provision` IS A Provision @desc What you want your will to do
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my will make this provision` IF
    CONSIDER `the provision`
    WHEN `a gift of property I am not entitled to when I die` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `7 -- am I old enough`

-- s 7: "Subject to section 17, a will made by a person under the age of 18 years is not valid."
-- There is no exception for a married minor and no leave of a court. s 7 is not made subject to Part X, so (on the text) a
-- document the Supreme Court would accept under s 32 is still not valid if its maker was under 18.
-- s 17 saves only a will made under the repealed s 18 before the 2007 repeal (a "privileged" will).
@export Am I old enough to make a valid will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 7)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the date I made the will` IS A DATE @desc The date you signed (or made) the will
      `a privileged will saved by section 17` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made under the former s 18 (soldiers', sailors' and airmen's wills) before its repeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I old enough to make a valid will` IF
        `age in completed years` `my date of birth` `the date I made the will` AT LEAST `the age of capacity`
    OR  `a privileged will saved by section 17`

§§ `8, 11 -- how my will must be signed and witnessed`

-- s 8: "Subject to sections 17 and 20 and Parts XA, X and XI, a will is not valid unless -- (a) it is in writing; and (b) it is
-- signed by the testator or signed in the testator's name by some other person in the testator's presence and by the
-- testator's direction, in such place on the will so that it is apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended
-- to give effect by the signature to the writing signed as the testator's will; and (c) the testator makes or acknowledges
-- the signature in the presence of at least 2 witnesses present at the same time; and (d) the witnesses attest and subscribe
-- the will in the presence of the testator but no publication or form of attestation is necessary."
-- s 11: "A person who is unable to see and attest that a testator has signed a document cannot act as a witness to a will."
-- Nothing in s 8 or s 11 disqualifies a beneficiary, or a beneficiary's spouse, as a witness. The last field is recorded so
-- that the cases can show it changes nothing.
DECLARE `My will's signing` HAS
    `it is in writing`                                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 8(a)
    `I signed it`                                               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 8(b)
    `someone signed it for me, in my presence and at my direction` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 8(b)
    `the signature shows I meant it to give effect to the will` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 8(b): placed so this is "apparent on the face"
    `witnesses present together when I signed or acknowledged`  IS A NUMBER   -- s 8(c)
    `of them, unable to see and attest that I signed`           IS A NUMBER   -- s 11
    `the witnesses signed in my presence`                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 8(d): "attest and subscribe"
    `a witness, or a witness's spouse, takes a gift under it`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- read by nothing: see s 13 below

@export May this person act as a witness to my will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 11)?
GIVEN `able to see and attest that I signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person can see, and attest, that you signed
      `takes a gift under my will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person (or their spouse) is a beneficiary under your will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person witness my will` IF
    `able to see and attest that I signed`

@export Is my will signed and witnessed as Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 8 requires?
GIVEN `the signing` IS A `My will's signing` @desc How your will was signed and witnessed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my will signed and witnessed as section 8 requires` IF
        "s 8(a)" ... `the signing`'s `it is in writing`
    ... "s 8(b)" ... (`the signing`'s `I signed it` OR `the signing`'s `someone signed it for me, in my presence and at my direction`)
    ... "s 8(b)" ... `the signing`'s `the signature shows I meant it to give effect to the will`
    ... "s 8(c), s 11" ... `the signing`'s `witnesses present together when I signed or acknowledged` MINUS `the signing`'s `of them, unable to see and attest that I signed` AT LEAST `the number of witnesses required`
    ... "s 8(d)" ... `the signing`'s `the witnesses signed in my presence`

§§ `13 (deleted) -- does a witness lose a gift`

-- The plan for this encoding expected s 13 to be the rule on gifts to witnesses. In the deposit there is no s 13 text: the
-- body shows only "[Part IV: s. 12 deleted: No. 27 of 2007 s. 11; s. 13 deleted: No. 28 of 2003 s. 211(1)]". What the
-- deleted s 13 provided is NOT in the deposit, so it is not encoded (the lives call it "gifts to witnesses").
-- Other notes, note 1, sets out the Acts Amendment (Equality of Status) Act 2003 s 211(2): "The repeal effected by
-- subsection (1) applies to and in respect of the will of any person dying on or after the date of the commencement of this
-- section, whether the will was made or executed before, on or after that date but does not apply to the will of a person
-- who died before that date." Commencement: 1 July 2003 (compilation table).
-- So for any will-maker who dies on or after 1 July 2003, nothing left in this Act takes a gift away from a witness: s 8
-- counts witnesses, s 11 disqualifies only one who cannot see and attest.
DECLARE `What happens to a witness's gift` IS ONE OF
    `nothing in this Act takes the gift away`
    `the deleted section 13 may still apply, and its text is not in the deposit: not covered by this encoding`

@export Does a witness who takes a gift under my will lose it (Wills Act 1970 (WA), s 13 deleted)?
GIVEN `the date the will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died (while the will-maker is living, any later date, such as today)
GIVETH A `What happens to a witness's gift`
DECIDE `Does a witness lose a gift under my will` IS
    IF `is on or after` `the date the will-maker died` `the day section 13 was repealed`
    THEN `nothing in this Act takes the gift away`
    ELSE `the deleted section 13 may still apply, and its text is not in the deposit: not covered by this encoding`

§§ `17, 20, Parts XA, X and XI -- the other routes to a valid will`

-- s 20(1): "A will is taken to be properly executed if its execution conforms to the internal law in force in the place --
-- (a) where it was executed; or (b) that was the testator's domicile or habitual residence, either at the time the will was
-- executed, or at the testator's death; or (c) of which the testator was a national, either at the date of execution of the
-- will, or at the testator's death." s 20(2)(b): "a will, so far as it disposes of immovable property, if it has been
-- executed in conformity with the internal law in force in the place where the property is situated".
-- Part XA: s 32B gives the Annex "the force of law"; Annex Art 1: a will "shall be valid as regards form ... if it is made in
-- the form of an international will complying with the provisions set out in Articles 2 to 5".
-- Part XI: s 40(4) a court-authorised will "must be signed by the Principal Registrar and sealed with the seal of the Court";
-- s 39(2) takes it to be executed in a manner permitted by this Act.
-- s 17(2): a will made under the repealed s 18 "has effect as if the repeal had not occurred".
-- Whether a foreign law is complied with is a borrowed question, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Another route` HAS
    `it conforms to the law of where it was signed, or of my domicile, habitual residence or nationality` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 20(1)
    `it conforms to the law of where the land or building is`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 20(2)(b)
    `it is an international will under Annex Articles 2 to 5`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- Part XA
    `it is a statutory will signed by the Principal Registrar and sealed` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 40(4), s 39(2)
    `it is a privileged will saved by section 17`               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 17

@export Is my will taken to be properly executed under another place's law (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 20)?
GIVEN `the route` IS AN `Another route` @desc The other laws and forms your will may conform to
      `as to land or a building` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the question is about immovable property (land or a building) the will disposes of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my will properly executed under section 20` IF
        `the route`'s `it conforms to the law of where it was signed, or of my domicile, habitual residence or nationality`
    OR  (`as to land or a building` AND `the route`'s `it conforms to the law of where the land or building is`)

-- s 32(2): "A document purporting to embody the testamentary intentions of a deceased person, even though it has not been
-- executed in the manner required by this Act, constitutes -- (a) a will of the person ... if the Supreme Court is satisfied
-- that the person intended the document to constitute the person's will". s 32(1): "document" includes anything from which
-- "sounds, images or writings can be reproduced" (so a video or a text message may be a document).
-- s 33(1): s 32 "applies only to persons dying on or after the day on which section 23 of the Wills Amendment Act 2007 comes
-- into operation". The Court's satisfaction is a judgment handed to the Court, asked here as a fact.
DECLARE `What becomes of my informal document` IS ONE OF
    `it is my will, because the Supreme Court is satisfied I intended it to be (s 32)`
    `not while I am living: s 32 applies only to the document of a deceased person`
    `not a will: the Supreme Court is not satisfied I intended it to be`
    `not a will: s 33 confines s 32 to deaths on or after 9 February 2008`

@export Can a document I did not sign and have witnessed take effect as my will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) ss 32, 33)?
GIVEN `the will-maker has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person whose document it is has died
      `the date the will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date of death (ignored while the person is living)
      `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is (or would be) satisfied that the person intended the document to be their will
GIVETH A `What becomes of my informal document`
DECIDE `Can my informal document be my will` IS
    BRANCH
      IF (NOT `the will-maker has died`)
        THEN `not while I am living: s 32 applies only to the document of a deceased person`
      IF NOT (`is on or after` `the date the will-maker died` `the day the Wills Amendment Act 2007 commenced`)
        THEN `not a will: s 33 confines s 32 to deaths on or after 9 February 2008`
      IF `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will`
        THEN `it is my will, because the Supreme Court is satisfied I intended it to be (s 32)`
      OTHERWISE `not a will: the Supreme Court is not satisfied I intended it to be`

@export Is my will valid as to form, by s 8 or by one of the other routes (Wills Act 1970 (WA) ss 8, 17, 20, Parts XA, X, XI)?
GIVEN `the signing` IS A `My will's signing` @desc How your will was signed and witnessed
      `the route` IS AN `Another route` @desc The other laws and forms your will may conform to
      `as to land or a building` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the question is about land or a building the will disposes of
      `the will-maker has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker has died
      `the date the will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date of death (ignored while the will-maker is living)
      `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied, under s 32, that the will-maker intended the document as their will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my will formally valid` IF
        `Is my will signed and witnessed as section 8 requires` `the signing`
    OR  `Is my will properly executed under section 20` `the route` `as to land or a building`
    OR  `the route`'s `it is an international will under Annex Articles 2 to 5`
    OR  `the route`'s `it is a statutory will signed by the Principal Registrar and sealed`
    OR  `the route`'s `it is a privileged will saved by section 17`
    OR  `Can my informal document be my will` `the will-maker has died` `the date the will-maker died` `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will`
            EQUALS `it is my will, because the Supreme Court is satisfied I intended it to be (s 32)`

@export Is my will valid, as to my age and its form (Wills Act 1970 (WA) ss 7, 8 and the other routes)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The will-maker's date of birth
      `the date I made the will` IS A DATE @desc The date the will was made
      `the signing` IS A `My will's signing` @desc How the will was signed and witnessed
      `the route` IS AN `Another route` @desc The other laws and forms the will may conform to
      `as to land or a building` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the question is about land or a building the will disposes of
      `the will-maker has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker has died
      `the date the will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date of death (ignored while the will-maker is living)
      `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied under s 32
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my will valid` IF
        `Am I old enough to make a valid will` `my date of birth` `the date I made the will` (`the route`'s `it is a privileged will saved by section 17`)
    AND `Is my will formally valid` `the signing` `the route` `as to land or a building` `the will-maker has died` `the date the will-maker died` `the Supreme Court is satisfied the person intended it as their will`

§§ `10 -- does my later change to the will count`

-- s 10(2): "Subject to Part X, an alteration to a will after it has been executed is not effective unless the alteration is
-- executed in a manner in which a will is required or permitted to be executed by this Act." (3): "Subsection (2) does not
-- apply to an alteration to a will made by, or at the direction of, the testator if the words or effect of the will are no
-- longer apparent because of the alteration." (1): "apparent means legible by the unaided eye or with the help of a
-- magnifying lens but not otherwise".
@export Is my change to my will after signing it effective (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 10)?
GIVEN `I signed and had the change witnessed as for a will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the alteration itself was executed as a will must be
      `I made it, or directed it, and the original words can no longer be read even with a magnifying lens` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the alteration obliterates the words so they are no longer apparent
      `the Supreme Court accepts it under Part X` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied, after your death, that you intended the alteration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my alteration effective` IF
        `I signed and had the change witnessed as for a will`
    OR  `I made it, or directed it, and the original words can no longer be read even with a magnifying lens`
    OR  `the Supreme Court accepts it under Part X`

§§ `14 -- does my marriage revoke my will`

-- s 14(1): "A will is revoked by the marriage of the testator except where -- (a) it is made in contemplation of the marriage;
-- or (b) the will is made in exercise of a power of appointment where the property thereby appointed would not in default of
-- appointment pass to the testator's personal representatives as such." (3): a will is made in contemplation of a marriage if
-- "(a) it is expressed to be made in contemplation of the marriage; or (b) there is other evidence establishing" it.
-- s 4: "marriage" has its meaning in the Marriage Act 1961 (Cth) -- a borrowed term, asked as a fact.
@export Does my marriage revoke my will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 14)?
GIVEN `I married after making the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you married after you made the will
      `the will says it is made in contemplation of that marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of the marriage
      `other evidence shows it was made in contemplation of that marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is other evidence that the will was made in contemplation of the marriage
      `the will only appoints property that would not go to my personal representatives` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is an appointment under a power whose property would not, in default, pass to your personal representatives
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my marriage revoke my will` IF
        `I married after making the will`
    AND (NOT `the will says it is made in contemplation of that marriage`)
    AND (NOT `other evidence shows it was made in contemplation of that marriage`)
    AND (NOT `the will only appoints property that would not go to my personal representatives`)

-- s 14(2): "A will made in contemplation of the marriage of the testator is void if the marriage is not solemnised, unless the
-- will provides to the contrary."
@export Is my will void because the marriage I made it for did not happen (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 14(2))?
GIVEN `made in contemplation of a marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in contemplation of a marriage
      `the marriage was solemnised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that marriage took place
      `the will provides to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will says it stands even if the marriage does not happen
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my will void because the marriage did not happen` IF
        `made in contemplation of a marriage`
    AND (NOT `the marriage was solemnised`)
    AND (NOT `the will provides to the contrary`)

§§ `14A -- does the end of my marriage revoke my will`

-- s 14A(2): "A will is revoked by the ending of the testator's marriage except where -- (a) a contrary intention appears in the
-- will; or (b) there is other evidence establishing such an intention." (3): a marriage ends "(a) when a divorce order
-- terminating the marriage takes effect under the Family Law Act; or (b) on the granting of a decree of nullity ...; or (c) on
-- the dissolution or annulment of the marriage in accordance with the law of a place outside Australia, but only if that
-- dissolution or annulment is recognised in Australia under the Family Law Act". (4): s 14A(2) applies where the marriage ends
-- on or after the commencement of s 13 of the Wills Amendment Act 2007, and "does not apply where a marriage ends before that
-- day". Separation is not on the list, and neither is the death of the spouse.
DECLARE `What happened to my marriage` IS ONE OF
    `a divorce order took effect`                         -- s 14A(3)(a)
    `a decree of nullity was granted`                     -- s 14A(3)(b)
    `a foreign dissolution or annulment recognised in Australia` -- s 14A(3)(c)
    `a foreign dissolution not recognised in Australia`   -- excluded by s 14A(3)(c)
    `we separated, with no divorce order`                 -- not an ending
    `my spouse died`                                      -- not an ending

@export Has my marriage ended, for Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 14A?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened to my marriage` @desc What happened to your marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my marriage ended for section 14A` IF
    CONSIDER `what happened`
    WHEN `a divorce order took effect` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a decree of nullity was granted` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a foreign dissolution or annulment recognised in Australia` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Does the end of my marriage revoke my will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 14A)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened to my marriage` @desc What happened to your marriage
      `the date it happened` IS A DATE @desc The date the divorce order took effect (or the other event happened)
      `the will shows a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will says it is to stand despite the marriage ending
      `other evidence shows that intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether other evidence shows you meant the will to stand despite the marriage ending
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the end of my marriage revoke my will` IF
        `Has my marriage ended for section 14A` `what happened`
    AND `is on or after` `the date it happened` `the day the Wills Amendment Act 2007 commenced`
    AND (NOT `the will shows a contrary intention`)
    AND (NOT `other evidence shows that intention`)

§§ `15 -- how else I can revoke my will`

-- s 15: "Without limiting sections 14(1) and 14A(2), the whole or any part of a will may be revoked only -- (a) by a later will,
-- including a document that is a will by operation of Part X; or (b) subject to Part X, by some writing declaring an intention
-- to revoke it, executed in the manner in which a will is required or permitted to be executed by this Act; or (c) by the
-- testator, or some person in the testator's presence and by the testator's direction, burning, tearing or otherwise destroying
-- it to give effect to the intention of the testator of revoking it."
-- A later will revokes only so far as it does so: the encoding takes "a later will that revokes it" as the fact.
DECLARE `What I did` IS ONE OF
    `I made a later will that revokes it`                                      -- s 15(a)
    `a later document the Supreme Court accepts as my will under Part X revokes it` -- s 15(a)
    `I signed a writing revoking it, witnessed as for a will`                  -- s 15(b)
    `I burned, tore or destroyed it, meaning to revoke it`                     -- s 15(c)
    `someone destroyed it in my presence, at my direction, meaning to revoke it` -- s 15(c)
    `someone destroyed it at my direction, but not in my presence`             -- fails s 15(c)
    `it was destroyed by accident`                                             -- fails s 15(c): no intention to revoke
    `I wrote an unwitnessed note saying it is revoked`                         -- fails s 15(b), unless Part X
    `I told my family it is revoked`                                           -- not one of the s 15 ways

@export Does what I did revoke my will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 15)?
GIVEN `the act` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did to revoke the will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does what I did revoke my will` IF
    CONSIDER `the act`
    WHEN `someone destroyed it at my direction, but not in my presence` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `it was destroyed by accident` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `I wrote an unwitnessed note saying it is revoked` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `I told my family it is revoked` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `16 -- can I bring a revoked will back`

-- s 16(1): "Subject to Part X, a will or any part of a will that has been revoked is revived only by the reexecution of that
-- will or by execution of a later will, including a document that is a will by operation of Part X, showing an intention to
-- revive that will or part."
@export Can I bring my revoked will back to life this way (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 16(1))?
GIVEN `I re-executed the revoked will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed and had the old will witnessed again
      `I executed a later will showing an intention to revive it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a later will (or a document accepted under Part X) shows you meant to revive the old will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my revoked will revived` IF
        `I re-executed the revoked will`
    OR  `I executed a later will showing an intention to revive it`

§§ `26(1)(b), (g), 27 -- what becomes of a gift when the beneficiary dies first`

-- s 27(1): "Unless the contrary intention appears by the will, where -- (a) there is a disposition in a will to a person who is
-- a child or other issue of the testator, for an estate or interest not determinable at or before the death of that person;
-- and (b) that person dies in the lifetime of the testator leaving a child or children who survive the testator, the
-- disposition does not lapse but takes effect as a substitutional disposition to such of the children of that person as
-- survive the testator and if more than one in equal shares." (3): "This section does not apply to a disposition to a person
-- as one of 2 or more joint tenants."
-- s 26(1)(g): "if any part of a disposition in fractional parts of the whole or of the residue of the estate of a testator
-- fails, the part that fails accrues to the part that does not fail".
-- s 26(1)(b): property in a disposition "that is void or fails to take effect is to be included in any residuary disposition".
-- What becomes of property the will does not dispose of is the Administration Act 1903 (intestacy), not this Act.
DECLARE `The gift` HAS
    `the beneficiary survived the will-maker`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the beneficiary is a child or other issue of the will-maker` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 27(1)(a)
    `the gift was not to end at or before the beneficiary's death` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 27(1)(a): "not determinable"
    `the beneficiary left a child who survived the will-maker`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 27(1)(b)
    `given to the beneficiary as a joint tenant`                IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 27(3)
    `a fractional share of the whole or residue, and another share takes effect` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 26(1)(g)
    `itself the residue or the whole estate`                    IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 26(1)(b)
    `the will has a residuary gift`                             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 26(1)(b)
    `the will shows a contrary intention`                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- ss 26(1), 27(1): "unless the contrary intention appears"

DECLARE `What becomes of the gift` IS ONE OF
    `the gift takes effect`
    `as the will itself provides`
    `it passes to the beneficiary's children who survive, in equal shares`
    `the share accrues to the other shares`
    `the gift falls into the residue`
    `it is not disposed of by the will`

@export Do I, as a child of a beneficiary who died before the will-maker, take my parent's gift (Wills Act 1970 (WA) s 27)?
GIVEN `the gift` IS A `The gift` @desc The gift in the will and what happened to the beneficiary
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the beneficiary's children take the gift under section 27` IF
        (NOT `the gift`'s `the beneficiary survived the will-maker`)
    AND `the gift`'s `the beneficiary is a child or other issue of the will-maker`
    AND `the gift`'s `the gift was not to end at or before the beneficiary's death`
    AND `the gift`'s `the beneficiary left a child who survived the will-maker`
    AND (NOT `the gift`'s `given to the beneficiary as a joint tenant`)
    AND (NOT `the gift`'s `the will shows a contrary intention`)

@export What becomes of a gift in the will (Wills Act 1970 (WA) ss 26(1)(b), (g), 27)?
GIVEN `the gift` IS A `The gift` @desc The gift in the will and what happened to the beneficiary
GIVETH A `What becomes of the gift`
DECIDE `What becomes of the gift in my will` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `the gift`'s `the beneficiary survived the will-maker`
        THEN `the gift takes effect`
      IF `the gift`'s `the will shows a contrary intention`
        THEN `as the will itself provides`
      IF `Do the beneficiary's children take the gift under section 27` `the gift`
        THEN `it passes to the beneficiary's children who survive, in equal shares`
      IF `the gift`'s `a fractional share of the whole or residue, and another share takes effect`
        THEN `the share accrues to the other shares`
      IF (NOT `the gift`'s `itself the residue or the whole estate`) AND `the gift`'s `the will has a residuary gift`
        THEN `the gift falls into the residue`
      OTHERWISE `it is not disposed of by the will`

-- A child left out. Nothing in this Act gives a child, or anyone else, a share the will does not give: there is no forced
-- share in the Act. A claim against the estate is the Family Provision Act 1972, and intestacy is the Administration Act 1903;
-- neither is encoded here. The input is asked so the question can be put; the answer does not turn on it.
@export Does the Wills Act 1970 (WA) give me a share of the estate that the will does not give me?
GIVEN `I am a child of the will-maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a child of the person who made the will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this Act give me a share the will does not` IF
    `I am a child of the will-maker` AND FALSE

§§ `40, 42 -- a will made for me if I lose capacity`

-- s 40(1): the Court may "make an order authorising -- (a) the making or alteration of a will in specific terms approved by the
-- Court ... on behalf of a person who lacks testamentary capacity". (2): not "unless, at the time when the order is made, the
-- person concerned -- (a) is living; and (b) has reached the age of 18 years". s 42(1): the Court "must refuse" unless
-- satisfied of (a) incapacity, (b) the will is one the person could make if not lacking capacity, (c) the applicant is
-- appropriate, (d) adequate steps for representation; s 42(2): it may refuse for any other reason. So this decides only
-- whether the Court MAY make the order; the Court's satisfaction is asked as a fact.
@export May the Supreme Court authorise a will on my behalf (Wills Act 1970 (WA) ss 40, 42)?
GIVEN `I am living` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person concerned is living when the order is made
      `my age when the order is made` IS A NUMBER @desc Age of the person concerned, in completed years, when the order is made
      `I lack testamentary capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person concerned lacks testamentary capacity
      `the Court is satisfied of section 42(1)(a) to (d)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied of each matter in s 42(1)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Court authorise a will for me` IF
        `I am living`
    AND `my age when the order is made` AT LEAST `the age of capacity`
    AND `I lack testamentary capacity`
    AND `the Court is satisfied of section 42(1)(a) to (d)`
